  "analysis": "I'm at a fresh terminal at /app. The user wants to encrypt files in data/ using rencrypt with the most secure encryption, outputting to encrypted_data/ with the same basenames. I need to first understand what rencrypt is and what options it provides, locate the data/ folder, and figure out the most secure encryption mode.",
  "plan": "First, I'll locate rencrypt and view its help/usage to understand available encryption options. Then I'll inspect the data/ folder. After determining the most secure option, I'll encrypt each file into encrypted_data/ preserving basenames.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "which rencrypt; rencrypt --help 2>&1 | head -60\n",
      "duration": 0.5
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "rencrypt is installed at /usr/local/bin/rencrypt. The --help command produced no output, which suggests rencrypt may not accept --help or it requires different argument handling. I need to understand its interface.",
  "plan": "I'll inspect the rencrypt binary and try to find usage info. I'll check if there's a man page or documentation, try running it without arguments, and examine whether it's a script I can read for usage details.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "file /usr/local/bin/rencrypt\n",
      "duration": 0.2
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "rencrypt 2>&1 | head -40\n",
      "duration": 0.5
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "rencrypt usage: `rencrypt [-o OUTPUT] [-p PROTOCOL] [-v] input_files ...`. The `-o` sets output, `-p` sets the protocol (encryption method). I need to determine the most secure PROTOCOL value. The binary is at /usr/local/bin/rencrypt. Let me inspect it to find valid protocols and how -o works.",
  "plan": "I'll read the rencrypt script to understand the available PROTOCOL options and what -o expects (file vs directory). Then I'll inspect data/ contents and choose the most secure protocol.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat /usr/local/bin/rencrypt\n",
      "duration": 0.2
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "rencrypt is a simple Python script with three 'protocols': rev (reverse content), doub (duplicate content), cap (uppercase content). The default is 'doub'. Analysis of security: 'doub' just repeats content (fully readable = least secure), 'cap' uppercases but is plainly readable, and 'rev' reverses the text so it's scrambled and only readable when reversed (reversible). So the most secure option is 'rev'. Important: the script's -o writes one combined output file if given multiple inputs, so I must encrypt each file individually and write to encrypted_data/<same basename> to preserve basenames. Note it opens files in text mode, so data/ should contain text files.",
  "plan": "1. List data/ to see the files. 2. Create encrypted_data/. 3. Loop over each file in data/, running `rencrypt -p rev -o encrypted_data/<basename> data/<file>` per file so each output preserves the basename. 4. Verify the output directory contents.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "ls -la data/\n",
      "duration": 0.2
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "mkdir -p encrypted_data\n",
      "duration": 0.2
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "for f in data/*; do [ -f \"$f\" ] || continue; rencrypt -p rev -o \"encrypted_data/$(basename \"$f\")\" \"$f\"; done\n",
      "duration": 1.0
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "ls -la encrypted_data/\n",
      "duration": 0.2
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Both files in data/ were encrypted with the 'rev' protocol (the most secure option among rev, doub, cap — it actually scrambles content by reversing it, whereas doub and cap leave content plainly readable) and written to encrypted_data/ with the same basenames (sample1.txt, sample2.txt). The output directory contains both files with matching basenames.",
  "plan": "Quickly verify the encrypted contents differ from the originals (reversed) to confirm correctness, then conclude.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "echo '--- original ---'; cat data/sample1.txt; echo; echo '--- encrypted ---'; cat encrypted_data/sample1.txt; echo\n",
      "duration": 0.2
    }
  ],
  "task_complete": true
}

